WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.892
MYK TAYLOR: So hello everyone.

00:00:09.892 --> 00:00:12.350
All right, everyone here looking
for a shrinking production

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:13.580
incidents?

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.800
We're going to talk
about how to keep outages

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.470
from affecting your users
and keeping them happy

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:22.500
and keeping everyone happy.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.580
So whenever you go to
a talk, you generally

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.640
want to know who is this person,
and why should I spend an hour

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:29.870
listening to them?

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:31.400
So who is Myk Taylor?

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.610
Well, he is me.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.360
I spent a decade or so building
architectures and services

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.297
for companies large and small.

00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:42.380
And then I got interested
in this newfangled thing

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:43.760
called DevOps.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.690
So I came on board
at Google as an SRE.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:49.790
And I've been in SRE
on-call for Google Cloud

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:52.850
for about three years now.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.290
And in those three
years, I think

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.920
I found more about preparing
for, mitigating, and avoiding

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.710
production incidents than I had
in my entire previous career.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.350
So I'm here with
you today to share

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.558
some of the best things I've
learned about production

00:01:05.558 --> 00:01:08.750
incidents or, as we
usually call them, outages

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.533
and how to shrink them.

00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:12.950
But first, it's
important to cover

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.350
why I'm talking about
production incidents

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.090
and how to shrink them.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.760
The head of Google Cloud
Customer Reliability

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.400
Engineering, Dave
Brinson, who you may

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.010
have seen walking around here--

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:26.840
he always says that reliability
is your most important feature

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.460
of any service.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.290
Users may not notice
when you have it.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.530
But boy, do they notice
when you don't have it.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.540
Every time your
service has an outage,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.870
you burn away some of
your users' goodwill.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.060
Do this too often,
and your service

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.180
will be too frustrating to use.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.680
And your users will
go somewhere else.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.690
If you want to keep
your users, even

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.360
if they're purely internal
to your own company,

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.920
reliability is key.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.010
And it's definitely not free.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.098
Reliability takes a lot of work.

00:01:57.098 --> 00:01:58.640
And I've not even
heard of a two nine

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.820
service, that just
99% availability.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:06.412
Not even that stays up without
constant engineering effort.

00:02:06.412 --> 00:02:08.870
So that's why we're going to
talk about today, what you can

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:12.240
do to keep things up and happy.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.320
So we've already established
user happiness is important.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.196
Let's graph it across the
timeline of an incident.

00:02:19.196 --> 00:02:21.930
So your service has a
level of reliability,

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:25.330
the border between the blue and
the red areas of the graph--

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:28.180
that's still kind of blue--

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.160
that, when you are
below it, your users

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.440
will be unhappy in some sense.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.432
Does anyone know what
this level is called?

00:02:36.432 --> 00:02:37.140
Anyone out there?

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:37.730
Have you heard of it?

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.313
I'm sure we've
mentioned it every day.

00:02:39.313 --> 00:02:40.070
AUDIENCE: SLO.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:43.150
MYK TAYLOR: SLO, that's right--
your service level objective.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.050
Five points to Gryffindor.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:49.190
That's an S just a little
bit off the screen there.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.980
Now, usually the
further below your SLO

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.190
that you go, the unhappier
your users become.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.710
Above the target
level for your SLO--

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.480
and this is really
the amazing part--

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.770
your users become
indifferent to reliability.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.590
You can be hovering just
above the red or way up

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.170
at the top of the graph,
and your users just

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.630
don't perceive any real
difference, at least

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:13.218
in the long run.

00:03:13.218 --> 00:03:15.260
I mean, you're not going
to stop having problems.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.420
And users will still
see those blips.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.010
But as long as, on average,
you're above that level,

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.460
then your users won't feel
like it's that bad of a deal.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.990
So the blue area up there, that
represents your error budget.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:33.910
When people say, don't
sweat the small stuff,

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:36.463
the small stuff is what fits
up there in your error budget.

00:03:36.463 --> 00:03:38.380
You can use it like what
Gary was just talking

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:39.753
about for fault injection.

00:03:39.753 --> 00:03:41.920
This is where you do your
testing, when you're still

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:42.880
up in that blue area.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.223
That's what you can use it for.

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:46.140
But that's kind of a
different topic from what

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.690
I'll be talking about.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:51.510
I'm focusing today on
keeping you out of the red.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.140
So what's happening
in this graph?

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:54.480
Well, not much yet.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.240
Let's say it's the user
happiness graph for a chat app

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.160
because Google loves
chat apps, and we love

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.500
building graphs for chat apps.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.020
So we start off nice and
high in the blue region

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:08.940
when, bam, an outage begins.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:12.750
Maybe the release we just pushed
is overloading a dependency.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.810
Maybe some developer added a
log statement to a hot path

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.420
and I/O was blocking.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:21.149
Something bad happened,
and it's getting worse.

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:23.770
Now, the SREs on
call for this service

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:25.320
are blissfully
unaware that anything

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.070
is wrong at this point.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:30.000
In fact, they just went
out to lunch as usual.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.830
In the meantime,
though, your users

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:33.930
are starting to get
a little annoyed.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.200
But their patience
hasn't run out yet.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.900
Once enough errors
have accumulated,

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.090
the alerting system
decides it's time to page

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:45.390
the on-call who checks her
phone, [? axe ?] the page,

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.460
stuffs a little more
food into her mouth,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:50.500
and hurries back to her desk
to find out what's going on.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.260
Now, this is a
particularly bad outage,

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.278
so we're starting to
dip below our SLO.

00:04:55.278 --> 00:04:56.820
And if you check
online, maybe you'll

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:00.615
start seeing a few complaints
pop up here and there.

00:05:00.615 --> 00:05:02.240
So it takes some time
for the on-caller

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.040
to figure out what's wrong.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:03.998
But they figure it out.

00:05:03.998 --> 00:05:05.410
They mitigate the problem.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:08.560
And then your service starts
running reliably again.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.630
So now your users are starting
to forget about the outage.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.840
It was bad, but
they got through it.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:14.850
And now things are OK.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.433
And the longer it
stays OK, the better

00:05:16.433 --> 00:05:18.640
they're going to feel about it.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.710
But it never stays that way.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:23.410
And once you have
another outage,

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.380
then you might take another hit.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:28.570
Now, of course,
you want your users

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.560
to have the best experience.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.980
So let's look at this
graph and break it down

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.230
to the parts you could improve.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.380
You want to minimize
your time to detect--

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.200
this part-- and the
time to repair--

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.440
this part-- while maximizing
the time between failures.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.830
Now, that's all pretty obvious.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.610
But think about how this graph
changes if the time to detect

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:53.950
or the time to
repair were shorter

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.650
or if the slope of the
graph during the outage

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.500
were less steep or
if you had longer

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.410
to recover before the
next outage began.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:05.710
You'd have much less danger
of dipping back into the red.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:07.720
This is what I mean
by shrinking here.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:11.530
If you reduce the duration,
impact, and frequency

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:14.450
of production incidents, it
helps keep your users out

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:15.450
of the unhappy red zone.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.670
So how do you know
how much you invest

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:23.620
for improving each phase?

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.900
Which phase do you target first?

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.310
And where do you get
the data for analysis?

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:30.610
So three is a magic
number, right?

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:32.500
These three things
underpin everything

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.143
I'm going to talk about today.

00:06:34.143 --> 00:06:36.310
Number one, of course-- and
you've heard a lot about

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:37.480
these--

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:38.200
SLOs.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.680
You have to create and
maintain SLOs for your service.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:44.050
They're the basis for
your error budgets.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:47.290
They define the desired
measurable reliability

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:49.090
of your service.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:51.228
And they affect the
entire incident cycle

00:06:51.228 --> 00:06:53.020
because they tell you
when something is bad

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:57.730
and, more importantly, they
tell you how bad it is.

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:01.190
Number two is postmortems.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:03.080
You have to write postmortems.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:05.380
Postmortems are all about
how to make things better

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:06.720
the next time.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.160
So when do you write one?

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.090
Every time your SLO takes
a hit, write a postmortem.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.430
Sometimes something
happens where you get lucky

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.982
and your SLO didn't take
a hit, but it could have.

00:07:16.982 --> 00:07:18.690
You still want to
write a postmortem then

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:20.760
because you don't want
to depend on luck to keep

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.080
your service reliable.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.100
It's the SRE motto.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:24.240
You see it everywhere.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.420
Hope is not a strategy.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.170
The reason we're talking
about postmortems

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:32.780
here is because they record
what happened, when it happened,

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.450
and why it happens.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:36.230
I'll be talking more
about them later.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:40.100
But this is a very fundamental
thing that we have here.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:42.910
And finally, if you
want accurate data,

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:47.510
it really, really helps have
a strong, blameless culture.

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:50.085
A blameless culture knows
that, whatever you did,

00:07:50.085 --> 00:07:51.460
there's probably
something better

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:53.132
that you could have done.

00:07:53.132 --> 00:07:54.340
Everybody's had that feeling.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.320
You're trying to
handle something--

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.300
hindsight is always
20/20, but you

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.450
don't have hindsight in
the middle of an outage.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.290
Whatever you do, it made
sense to you at the time.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.805
And you did the
best that you could.

00:08:06.805 --> 00:08:08.680
If it's hard for you to
figure out what to do

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:11.480
or if it's too easy for you to
do something counterproductive,

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:14.480
it's the system
that should change.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:17.350
You should not be held
responsible for doing the best

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.720
job that you could with the
information and the tools

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.090
that you had.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:24.220
A blameless culture
is essential so

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:28.420
that you aren't afraid to reach
out for help during the outage.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:33.033
And you can be honest and
open in the postmortem.

00:08:33.033 --> 00:08:34.450
This makes the
postmortem actually

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:36.632
useful as a learning tool.

00:08:36.632 --> 00:08:37.340
Here's the thing.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.370
If you don't have a
blameless culture,

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.020
then you're living
in fear of what?

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:42.220
Blame.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:44.770
Your primary motivation
becomes to avoid blame,

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:45.770
not to help your users.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:48.820
It's to protect yourself,
to protect your co-workers.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:51.640
Would you really dive in
and try to find a root cause

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.710
if you think that the
results of my research

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:54.743
could get someone fired?

00:08:54.743 --> 00:08:56.910
That's not the environment
that you want to live in.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.030
That's not going
to help anybody.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:01.690
So a blameless
culture, a postmortem

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:06.003
built on the blameless culture,
and SLOs to underpin it all.

00:09:06.003 --> 00:09:07.670
These are the three
fundamental pillars,

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:10.045
so remember these as we're
going through everything else.

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:15.240
OK, so you've got your SLOs.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:18.390
You know if you need to shrink
to keep your users happy.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:20.390
And you've got your
blameless postmortems.

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.560
So you have a clue
where you need to shrink

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.300
to keep your users happy.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:29.010
Now, what do you do to shrink
to keep your users happy?

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:31.810
You know overall what you
want the graph to look like.

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:34.170
But let's zoom in on
each phase and figure out

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.900
what kind of tasks we can
focus on to bring each of them

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:38.580
under control.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.490
Now, nothing I'm going to talk
about here is revolutionary.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:44.220
I'm sure everyone here has heard
of everything I'm going to say,

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.000
and you know they're
all important.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.880
But what I'm trying
to do here is

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:51.420
relate each task to which
phase they affect most so

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:54.060
that, once you figure out where
you need to help yourself,

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:56.560
you can then look and say, OK,
these are the kinds of things

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:57.480
that we can work on.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:01.680
And this is what's going to be
the best benefit to our team.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.270
What I want you to
think about as I

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.190
go through the
next few slides is

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:07.620
how is my service doing here?

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:09.790
How does this apply to me?

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:11.010
Where can I invest more?

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:12.665
Where is it going
to help me most?

00:10:12.665 --> 00:10:14.040
And at the end,
we'll have a Q&amp;A.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.300
And we can go deeper into
whatever questions you have.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.800
I should really note, though,
that even if you don't already

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:23.783
have a blameless culture or
postmortems or even a SLOs,

00:10:23.783 --> 00:10:25.200
all the stuff in
this presentation

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:27.180
is pretty fundamental.

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:30.660
So you can use it as a checklist
to get an instant readiness

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:32.700
assessment of your service.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:34.440
And they're all need to do.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:36.960
It's just how much you
need to do each one.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:40.350
And then you can use
future postmortems

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.180
to focus in on the ones
that help you most.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:48.230
All right, let's get started.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:50.620
So you're having an outage
when your error budget starts

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:53.710
burning so fast that,
if you ignore it,

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:55.990
it'll burn away all of
your remaining budget.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:57.910
You can see there
in the yellow box

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:00.580
that slope is definitely going
to intersect with the red

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:02.060
really soon.

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:05.950
So that's the condition that
you have to fire your alert on.

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:08.883
The time to detect is from
the start of the outage

00:11:08.883 --> 00:11:10.300
to when a person
that can actually

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:13.630
work on fixing the
problem is alerted.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:16.240
Remember, as a person
on-call for a service,

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:19.330
you can't even begin to think
about mitigating an issue

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:21.220
until you know it's there.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:22.960
So some proactive
work here is required.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:28.900
You really don't want your
first postmortem to say,

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:30.350
service died.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:31.420
Nobody knew.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:32.427
We lost a lot of money.

00:11:32.427 --> 00:11:33.510
It's not a good way to go.

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:38.910
All right, so we have
another good rule of three

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:41.850
here for shrinking
time to detect.

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:45.060
First off, you need a
good signal-to-noise ratio

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:46.440
for your SLI.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.180
That's your service
level indicator

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:49.810
that your SLO is based on.

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:54.150
You know whether you have a
problem because of your SLIs.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:57.540
Good SLIs only move up or down
when your users are actively

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.850
getting happier or sadder.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:01.950
If this isn't true for
the SLIs that you have,

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:06.430
you need to fix them
since you really

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:08.860
only want to alert on problems
that your users actually

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:09.860
care about.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:11.860
So make sure your
SLIs map very well

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.090
to how your users
are actually feeling.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:15.230
How do you do that?

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.350
Well, you have to
have some backup way

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:19.090
to figure out how your
users are feeling,

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.850
internet complaints,
service calls, phone

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:24.340
calls from angry
customers, something

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:27.310
to tell you that I'm in the
red and they're calling me.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:28.570
That's a good signal.

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:30.550
I'm in the red and
no one's calling me,

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:33.600
maybe your SLI is too sensitive.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:36.637
Maybe you have a lot
of jitter in your SLI

00:12:36.637 --> 00:12:38.220
and you can't use
it very effectively.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.422
A lot of people have
like a batch process

00:12:40.422 --> 00:12:42.630
that will hit their service
really hard for 5 minutes

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:43.873
or 10 minutes at midnight.

00:12:43.873 --> 00:12:46.290
You don't really want to have
a bunch of errors showing up

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:49.290
at midnight for a batch
process that you control

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:52.230
and have it page all the
people on your on-call service

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:53.070
at midnight.

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:56.430
So maybe take that traffic and
split it off to a separate SLI.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.460
Or some people
have SLIs that have

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.470
all errors who turn
in from a service

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:02.550
counts against their SLI.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:05.938
But 400s, 404s, maybe they
don't care about those.

00:13:05.938 --> 00:13:07.230
Maybe that's not a good signal.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:15.520
Where are we?

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.690
Yeah, so in the
beginning, for your SLIs,

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:20.450
it's most important just
to have something and then

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:22.190
iterate, iterate, iterate.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:24.830
Every time you have a
postmortem, look back on it

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:28.730
and think, how did my SLI
teach me about what's actually

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:30.710
going on in this outage?

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:32.810
Is it worse than
what the SLI said?

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:33.680
Tweak it.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:34.600
Is it better?

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:35.790
Go back the other way.

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:37.245
Am I measuring the wrong thing?

00:13:37.245 --> 00:13:38.620
Go back and make
a different SLI.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:47.460
All right, the next is--

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:49.460
so you have your SLI
actually reading something.

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:51.348
You actually have to
get your readings fresh

00:13:51.348 --> 00:13:53.390
so your learning system
can do something with it.

00:13:55.837 --> 00:13:58.420
This means that you actually get
your alerts before everything

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.300
explodes hopefully.

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:03.490
One company I worked for
generated all their metrics

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:04.602
from analyzing logs.

00:14:04.602 --> 00:14:06.310
Now, this worked out
pretty well for them

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:08.553
analyzing historic behavior.

00:14:08.553 --> 00:14:09.970
But it introduced
a lot of latency

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:11.860
for their actual alerts.

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:16.330
So we helped them move it from
a batch log processing system

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.820
to a streaming metrics
collection system that

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:22.330
goes directly from their service
process to the metrics back end

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:24.675
and is analyzed in line.

00:14:24.675 --> 00:14:26.800
And this helped them get
a lot of that latency out.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:32.040
So the final element to
shrinking time to detect

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.520
is the alerts themselves.

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:37.780
What does it mean for an
alert to be effective?

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:44.050
Effective alerts are user
focused, sensitive, actionable,

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:45.820
and delivered appropriately.

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:47.980
Now, I'll go through
that one by one.

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:51.040
You want user focused alerts
that only fire when users

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:52.773
are actually being affected.

00:14:52.773 --> 00:14:54.190
This is where your
SLO definitions

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:55.750
come into play like I said.

00:14:55.750 --> 00:14:59.320
If your SLOs are user
focused, then your SLIs

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:02.220
will be user focused and your
alerts will be user focused.

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.150
They all fit
together really well.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:06.490
If you base your SLI
on some indirect metric

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:09.190
like CPU utilization,
it's not a good signal.

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:11.882
It's a very low signal SLI.

00:15:11.882 --> 00:15:13.090
Sometimes it means something.

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:15.628
Sometimes it doesn't.

00:15:15.628 --> 00:15:19.750
You want your alerts to be
both sensitive and actionable.

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:22.390
But it's hard to get them
both at the same time.

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:25.180
Alert too quickly, and
you'll be paging at midnight

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:27.070
for something that
goes away in seconds.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:29.200
And your on-callers will
start looking at it,

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:30.825
and they'll see it's
already gone away.

00:15:30.825 --> 00:15:32.470
And they'll be annoyed.

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:34.245
But if you alert too
slowly, then you'll

00:15:34.245 --> 00:15:35.620
be wasting time
that you could be

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:38.290
using to mitigate real outages.

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:43.090
It takes some analysis and
time to find the right balance.

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:46.090
It's also important to make
sure that the alerts get

00:15:46.090 --> 00:15:47.592
to the right person.

00:15:47.592 --> 00:15:50.050
Smaller companies don't have
as much of a problem with this

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:52.120
if they only have
one on-call team.

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:54.590
But larger companies that
have multiple on-call teams,

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:56.840
you need to make sure you
loop in all the right people

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:58.450
as fast as you can.

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:01.150
If you can do any kind of
automated root cause analysis

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:03.700
and automatically bring
those people into the alert,

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:05.200
then that will save
a lot of minutes

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:07.930
as you're trying to
get things figured out.

00:16:07.930 --> 00:16:11.770
Finally, and this is
important, make it loud.

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:15.790
Use a service like PagerDuty,
opsgenie, VictorOps, Fire

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:18.040
Hydrant, something
that sends alerts

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:19.960
as a phone call or a page.

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:21.400
Don't just use email.

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:24.740
I repeat, do not just use email.

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:26.838
Email is to be ignored.

00:16:26.838 --> 00:16:27.880
That's all it's good for.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:30.580
I repeat, it is not good
at all to use email.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.250
The time will come when the
person on-call for your service

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:37.060
has got to take a shower or
eat dinner or go to sleep.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:41.270
And very few people check their
email while doing these things.

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:43.140
So with these three,
your time to detect

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:44.390
would be in pretty good shape.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:53.130
So shrinking time to detect
was all technical tasks.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:55.550
Shrinking time to
repair, though,

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:59.070
is mostly about people,
policies, training, and, most

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:01.950
importantly, stress management.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:04.060
Even if you alert
super efficiently,

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:07.680
What good is it if everybody
panics when you get the alert?

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:10.819
In my opinion, this is the most
important section to focus on.

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:12.150
You'll always have outages.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:13.680
They're not going away.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:15.630
You can automate fast
solutions to many

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.700
of these but not all of them.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:21.672
You hire engineers because
they're smart and creative.

00:17:21.672 --> 00:17:23.339
And they're ultimately
the best resource

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:26.790
you have for fixing the problems
that slip through the cracks.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.550
Of course, they're also
the slowest mechanism

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:31.540
you have for fixing
the problems.

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:35.270
But they're your last,
most capable defense.

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:38.380
And it pays to
prepare them properly.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:41.010
So a quick reminder before
we begin this section.

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:42.750
An incident
responder's first duty

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:45.720
is to protect the user
and the user's data.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:49.260
During your outage, keep
your focus just on that.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:52.500
Don't go and try finding
all the causal factors.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:54.390
It just doesn't
help at that point.

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:57.360
So a good response is a
temporary failover, a version

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.730
rollback, an emergency
request for more resources.

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:01.680
That's all good.

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:03.570
A bad response--
go into the code,

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:06.570
debug it, fix it, check it
in, build a new release,

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:07.320
push it out.

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:08.770
That takes way too long.

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:10.128
You don't have time for this.

00:18:10.128 --> 00:18:12.420
Do that later when the service
is not actively burning.

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:18.830
So what can you do to
improve your time to repair?

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:25.050
First, you have to
train your responders.

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:28.013
You need emergency
procedures defined and tested

00:18:28.013 --> 00:18:30.180
so that everyone involved
can be calm and productive

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:32.460
during an outage.

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.740
You need procedures that
provide enough structure so

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:37.830
that it's all organized and
people know what's going on.

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:39.660
But you need them to
be flexible enough so

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:43.320
that a lot of ad hoc debugging
and impromptu substructures

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:45.800
can take effect.

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:47.930
And don't just train
the people on call.

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:50.000
Train everybody who might
participate in incident

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:53.720
response-- devs, product
managers, project managers,

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:56.480
even CEOs, anybody who
might contribute and have

00:18:56.480 --> 00:18:58.430
to make some sort of call.

00:18:58.430 --> 00:19:00.590
And don't train them just once.

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:04.190
Instant response training gets
rusty really, really quickly.

00:19:04.190 --> 00:19:06.350
To keep it fresh, a
lot of teams at Google

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:08.410
run weekly failure drills.

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:10.040
We call them wheels
of misfortune

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:11.540
because we choose
somebody at random

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:14.190
and make them try something
like fix some sort of problem

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:16.190
in production.

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:17.572
We call them volunteers.

00:19:17.572 --> 00:19:19.280
But we put that in
quotes because they're

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.800
more voluntold what to do.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:24.800
So they go, and they're
given some sort of alert.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:27.470
And they talk through the
steps what they would do.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:28.970
And then another
member of the team,

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:30.830
someone who organized
the drill, will

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:34.400
play the part of any external
systems or external teams

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:36.620
that are contacting with them.

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:38.150
A simple example
could be an alert

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:40.520
comes in that users can't
log into your service.

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:41.610
What do you do?

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:43.760
And you go talk through
all the tools that you use,

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:45.843
all the things you
investigate, and all the people

00:19:45.843 --> 00:19:47.700
that you contact.

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:50.510
You can also hold practical
disaster recovery tests

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:53.272
where you actually engineer
real, controlled outages

00:19:53.272 --> 00:19:54.230
and then mitigate them.

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:55.688
This is a lot what
Gary was talking

00:19:55.688 --> 00:19:57.657
about in the last presentation.

00:19:57.657 --> 00:19:59.990
This gives everyone practice
with actually communicating

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:02.160
with each other and,
among other things,

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:05.330
tests all your
escalation channels.

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:07.010
You can get the whole
company together

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:10.450
to do a whole company
drill like this.

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:12.020
A scenario that
Google likes to run

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:14.300
is pretend that California
had a major earthquake

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:15.930
and slipped into the ocean.

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:18.565
So Google headquarters
is just gone.

00:20:18.565 --> 00:20:19.940
What does the rest
of the company

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:21.930
do to continue functioning?

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:23.900
And we this almost
every year because it's

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:25.760
important to figure
out how you continue

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:30.450
working if your headquarters
just disappeared.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:33.660
Number two here is that you
have to write alert runbooks.

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:36.050
Some people call them playbooks.

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:38.160
A runbook is the first
thing that an on-caller

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.030
sees when they get an alert.

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:46.270
You need to write one for
every alert that can fire.

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:48.940
Write down things like
special response procedures,

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:51.670
links to dashboards,
what queries

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:54.290
you would run in the debug
logs to find more information.

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:57.280
Everything someone who's
trying to handle the alert

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:58.865
would like to know.

00:20:58.865 --> 00:21:01.240
I'm not saying that you need
to know every possible cause

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:03.252
and fix before you
even write an alert.

00:21:03.252 --> 00:21:04.960
But the runbook should
give you some sort

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:07.458
of clue for how to
start the investigation

00:21:07.458 --> 00:21:08.500
and where to go for help.

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:12.800
Now, something I
see a lot is someone

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:16.340
writes a runbook but then
nobody else can understand it.

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:17.558
Don't do this.

00:21:17.558 --> 00:21:19.100
When you write a
runbook, even if you

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:21.300
think you're just
writing it for yourself,

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:24.133
keep it brain dead simple.

00:21:24.133 --> 00:21:25.550
Write it for a
version of yourself

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:27.740
that just got back from
vacation and has completely

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:30.992
forgotten how to do everything.

00:21:30.992 --> 00:21:32.450
This is actually
a good opportunity

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:33.920
for a failure drill.

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:36.610
Give the runbook to an alert--

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:39.620
give an alert for the
runbook to a volunteer

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:41.060
and see if they can follow it.

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:42.477
Is there anything
that they needed

00:21:42.477 --> 00:21:44.030
to do that wasn't
in the runbook?

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:45.530
Add it to the runbook.

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:48.620
It's just way too easy to
forget simple basic steps

00:21:48.620 --> 00:21:51.540
in the stress of an outage.

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:56.210
So next up-- you need
to create a pager SLO.

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:58.670
So this is different
from your service SLO.

00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:00.170
This is the policy
for how long it

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:04.640
can take for the person on call
to start working on a page.

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:06.980
Regardless of what you
actually set it to,

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:09.180
it'll help even out
your time to repair.

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:11.430
It'll become more consistent,
and that's a good thing.

00:22:11.430 --> 00:22:13.820
But what do you set it to?

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:16.160
It's easy to think,
duh, just start working

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:17.680
as soon as you get paged.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:19.610
But it's not quite that simple.

00:22:19.610 --> 00:22:21.680
Say you set your
pager's SLO to something

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:23.270
short like five minutes.

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:26.420
Excellent, your average time
to repair just went down.

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:29.500
But what's the cost?

00:22:29.500 --> 00:22:31.310
It's easy to meet
a five minute pager

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:33.390
SLO when you're at the office.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:35.960
But what about the
evenings, the weekends?

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:38.810
If you're on call, you can never
be more than five minutes away

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:40.550
from your work computer.

00:22:40.550 --> 00:22:41.600
Grocery shopping?

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:42.620
No way.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:45.080
Going out to the park for
a picnic with your family?

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:46.130
It's not going to happen.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:47.810
You can bring your
laptop, but you

00:22:47.810 --> 00:22:49.352
have to think--
what's it going to be

00:22:49.352 --> 00:22:52.580
like trying to solve a real
problem with your laptop,

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:55.160
going through your phone, and
going over a slow connection?

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.660
It's not fun.

00:22:57.660 --> 00:22:59.730
There are some teams at
Google that do actually

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:01.530
have a five minute pager SLO.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:04.990
But many teams find that 30
minutes is more reasonable.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:08.490
Think of your pager SLO
as your jumpiness factor.

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:11.460
How jumpy do you need
your on-call team to be?

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:12.655
Choose this carefully.

00:23:12.655 --> 00:23:14.280
It's going to determine
how quickly you

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.108
get started on
incidents, but it's also

00:23:16.108 --> 00:23:17.400
going to affect people's lives.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:27.760
In the same theme, actually
very much the same theme,

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:31.510
you want to start to
reduce responder fatigue.

00:23:31.510 --> 00:23:34.900
As you might guess,
burnout is a real problem.

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:37.780
Being on call is
a hard job to do.

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:40.330
If you want to keep your
on-callers on the team

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:41.830
that they're on
call for, you better

00:23:41.830 --> 00:23:44.960
make their happiness a priority.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:47.110
You can automate routine
operational tasks.

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:49.540
This takes a lot of the load
off their everyday life.

00:23:49.540 --> 00:23:51.790
We call this eliminating toil.

00:23:51.790 --> 00:23:54.490
A classic example of this
is release management.

00:23:54.490 --> 00:23:56.380
How much of the release
can you automate

00:23:56.380 --> 00:23:58.630
so that you don't have to
babysit it and click buttons

00:23:58.630 --> 00:24:00.670
every half an hour?

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:02.840
You can get more
people on the rotation

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:06.310
so your turn for on call
doesn't come up as often.

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:08.890
At Google, there's a general
rule for six people minimum

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:10.010
on rotation.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:12.360
You might not actually
have that many SREs.

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:15.460
But if you're short on that,
then pull in some devs.

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:18.250
Some senior developers make
really good on-callers.

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:20.480
And remember,
you're never alone.

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:22.730
So if the primary on-caller
doesn't know what to do,

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:26.760
there's always someone else on
backup that they can contact.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:27.900
This is always a fun one.

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:30.240
Get rid of unactionable alerts.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:31.950
You can tune them to
be more effective.

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:34.060
But maybe you can
just delete them.

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:37.170
I can tell you that there's no
greater joy in an SRE's life

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:40.810
than proving an alert is useless
and then going and deleting it.

00:24:40.810 --> 00:24:42.330
We always throw a
little celebration

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:45.440
whenever that happens.

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:48.680
And finally, you might be
able to relax your SLOs.

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:50.275
This can be your
pager SLO so that you

00:24:50.275 --> 00:24:51.900
don't have to be so
jumpy all the time,

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:53.660
even when you're
not getting pages.

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:56.250
Or it could be your service SLO.

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:59.150
Do you really need
all five nines?

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:00.170
Maybe not.

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:02.090
Five nines is hard to hit.

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:04.372
Maybe four nines, 3 and 1/2.

00:25:04.372 --> 00:25:06.080
Anything that brings
it down a little bit

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:07.728
and still keeps your
users happy will

00:25:07.728 --> 00:25:09.770
make life a lot more
bearable for the people that

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:10.562
have to support it.

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:18.410
But wait, there's more.

00:25:18.410 --> 00:25:21.040
And this one's important, too.

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:24.220
You need to make sure your
responders are empowered

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:26.530
to do the job that
they're paid to do,

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:29.230
both technically
and politically.

00:25:29.230 --> 00:25:31.000
What do I mean by this?

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:34.690
You should make your on-callers
have authority during outages.

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:39.080
Give them control over the
system that they have to fix.

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:44.800
You don't want the outages to
go on longer than they have to

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:46.810
because your on-callers
have to go call the CEO

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:49.000
and ask for permission
to do something.

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:53.440
Or go and try something that's
really important then find out

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:54.250
access denied.

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:56.950
And they have to go and find
out why their access is denied.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:59.917
It delays everything, and
it's not good for your users.

00:25:59.917 --> 00:26:01.750
And you also have to
make sure that everyone

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:03.970
else who's working
on the incident

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:05.770
understands that,
when an on-call asks

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:08.590
you to do something, you do it.

00:26:08.590 --> 00:26:11.560
The flipside of this is that
you need a solid audit logging

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:15.070
framework so everyone is still
accountable for their actions.

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:17.530
Remember, blameless
does not mean invisible.

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:23.090
Another way for you to
speed up your response

00:26:23.090 --> 00:26:25.790
is to improve your
recovery tools.

00:26:25.790 --> 00:26:27.920
If you know what you
need to do right away

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.470
but it takes forever to
get it done, then maybe you

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:32.120
need to work on this.

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:34.590
You can start by writing
tools for fast rollbacks

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:38.165
because that's the first
best thing that any incident

00:26:38.165 --> 00:26:40.790
responder can try to do is roll
back the version that just went

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:44.450
out because that very
often fixes the problem.

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:46.628
Or automated failover--
when something

00:26:46.628 --> 00:26:48.920
goes wrong at that particular
part of your application,

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:51.980
can you just failover to
your backup somewhere?

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:54.350
This is very dangerous
to do manually.

00:26:54.350 --> 00:26:56.790
You want to automate
this as best you can.

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.030
This comes up all the
time in postmortems.

00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:01.940
When you write about what you
could do better next time,

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:03.980
think about what
tools you've used

00:27:03.980 --> 00:27:05.660
and what processes
can be made faster.

00:27:08.540 --> 00:27:11.880
And you need good monitoring.

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:15.540
When you get a page, the first
place you go is the runbook.

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:18.390
But the second is
usually the monitoring.

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:21.900
You want to see visual
time series graphs for all

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:23.740
of your internal metrics.

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:25.170
These are not the SLIs.

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:28.590
This is stuff like
error rates to back end

00:27:28.590 --> 00:27:32.010
services, q depth,
[? memory ?] pressure,

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:34.680
all the technical
details of your system

00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:39.090
that give you a clue where to
bring your investigation next.

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:42.540
And finally, you need
queriable logging.

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:45.920
Like monitoring, you need it
when investigating an alert.

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:49.960
It should tell you the error
details for what's going wrong.

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:52.680
And if you ever can't
get the details this way,

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:54.720
say so in the postmortems
so the devs can go in

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:58.958
and add the lines so the
next time it won't be so bad.

00:27:58.958 --> 00:28:00.750
You have enough to make
sure that you don't

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:03.060
have too much logging, though.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:05.703
Every line that
you log has a cost.

00:28:05.703 --> 00:28:07.620
And you don't want to
overload whatever system

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:09.900
you're using on the back
end for logs management.

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:11.745
That can get very
expensive, too.

00:28:11.745 --> 00:28:13.620
So try not to go overboard
with your logging.

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:20.270
You also need to be aware
of user privacy issues.

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:23.300
You can't just log user
name and all the data

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:24.470
that goes along with that.

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:25.940
Talk to your legal
team about this

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:27.852
and find out what you
can actually record

00:28:27.852 --> 00:28:29.310
and how fast you
need to delete it.

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:31.370
This can be a really big issue.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:34.400
And it's obvious, but
I still have to say it.

00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:36.710
Do not write passwords to logs.

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:45.430
It never ends well

00:28:45.430 --> 00:28:46.080
OK.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:47.460
So you've mitigated your outage.

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:48.670
Good job.

00:28:48.670 --> 00:28:50.880
Your users are slowly
rebuilding their confidence

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:52.470
in your service.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:56.370
You want this phase to last
as long as possible, right?

00:28:56.370 --> 00:29:00.330
At Google we found that the
most common trigger of outages

00:29:00.330 --> 00:29:02.310
is a code or config change.

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:04.030
So to maximize time
between failures,

00:29:04.030 --> 00:29:06.180
just eliminate all
changes, right?

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:08.780
Well, that might work
for a little while.

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:11.190
And actually, sometimes
it's a really good idea

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:12.780
like, say, you have
a business that

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:16.230
gets almost all its revenue
in one particular weekend.

00:29:16.230 --> 00:29:18.510
Maybe don't make any
changes during that weekend.

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:19.680
Why take the risk?

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:21.820
There's no purpose.

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:24.690
But in general, you don't
want changes to stop.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.570
There are always features
to push, bugs to fix,

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:31.440
new hardware to support,
new threats to counter.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:33.480
Changes, however,
can be made safer.

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:35.100
And that's what we'll
talk about here.

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:37.680
But first, let me go back
on what I said a second ago.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:41.988
You don't necessarily want this
to last as long as possible.

00:29:41.988 --> 00:29:44.280
There are some companies I've
worked with that are just

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:46.090
laser focused on this section.

00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:47.460
They think, just no outages.

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:49.588
The best way to go
is just no outages.

00:29:49.588 --> 00:29:51.630
But it turned out it was
the wrong thing for them

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:52.420
to focus on.

00:29:52.420 --> 00:29:56.020
And it's probably the wrong
thing for you to focus on, too.

00:29:56.020 --> 00:29:58.050
I'm not saying, don't
focus on it at all.

00:29:58.050 --> 00:30:02.080
But do not focus on this to the
exclusion of everything else.

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:04.540
What happens if you go too
long without an outage?

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:06.750
Your on-callers get
out of practice.

00:30:06.750 --> 00:30:08.610
And when you finally
do have an outage--

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:10.830
and you will-- what do they do?

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:13.235
They have to go and look
back at the procedures.

00:30:13.235 --> 00:30:14.610
They don't have
the muscle memory

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:16.132
to figure out what to do.

00:30:16.132 --> 00:30:18.090
And everything ends up
being much worse than it

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:20.100
would have been otherwise.

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:22.890
Also companies that prioritize
time between failures

00:30:22.890 --> 00:30:25.500
too highly often slow
the release process

00:30:25.500 --> 00:30:26.790
down to a crawl.

00:30:26.790 --> 00:30:29.110
And this is bad for
a number of reasons.

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:31.380
It's bad for dev
velocity, and it's

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:34.020
really bad for response
time to market forces.

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:36.720
When things happen in
your competitor companies,

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:39.240
you won't be able to
respond as quickly.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:41.910
In general, it's better to
focus on shrinking your time

00:30:41.910 --> 00:30:44.880
to detect and your time to
repair and just take this

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:45.720
as you need it.

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:51.850
But that being said, most
of us don't have the problem

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:53.750
of having too few outages.

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:56.170
So we still need to
work on this stuff.

00:30:56.170 --> 00:30:59.980
Number one is our old friend,
the blameless postmortem.

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:02.350
Write one after every
incident to capture metrics

00:31:02.350 --> 00:31:04.245
and analyze how things went.

00:31:04.245 --> 00:31:05.620
And most importantly,
you discuss

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:07.120
action items to make it better.

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:10.885
And also, most importantly, make
sure you do the action items.

00:31:10.885 --> 00:31:12.760
These are just not to
be written and ignored.

00:31:12.760 --> 00:31:15.520
Make sure they get
prioritized very highly

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:19.548
against the other features
that devs are working on.

00:31:19.548 --> 00:31:21.840
Postmortems are the key to
preventing the same incident

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.810
from happening twice or
at least making it better

00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:26.370
when it happens again.

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:29.520
Remember, there is always going
to be new kinds of postmortems.

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:32.970
And you can't afford
to let them build up.

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:35.250
You should write your
postmortems collaboratively

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:36.708
with everyone else
that is involved

00:31:36.708 --> 00:31:38.583
to get all the different
perspectives and all

00:31:38.583 --> 00:31:39.752
the different viewpoints.

00:31:39.752 --> 00:31:41.460
And so everyone can
contribute to saying,

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:43.290
this is what I
thought was going on,

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:46.680
and this is why
I did what I did.

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:47.990
It's not that complicated.

00:31:47.990 --> 00:31:48.840
They sound hard.

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:50.257
But they're really
not bad at all.

00:31:50.257 --> 00:31:52.980
It's just a fact finding
document and a little bit of,

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:55.980
this is what I was thinking.

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:58.003
If you've seen the
Google SRE book online,

00:31:58.003 --> 00:31:59.670
there's a sample
postmortem in the back.

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:02.690
And you can use
that as a template.

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:05.225
Next up is CI/CD.

00:32:05.225 --> 00:32:07.100
You heard about this
earlier in the day, too.

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:10.610
That's continuous integration
and continuous delivery.

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:12.200
This is how you
can deploy changes

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:14.360
with reasonable confidence
that they won't blow

00:32:14.360 --> 00:32:16.560
up in [? prog, ?] probably.

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:17.810
Sometimes they blow up anyway.

00:32:17.810 --> 00:32:22.630
But this will help them become
smaller and less impactful.

00:32:22.630 --> 00:32:26.790
You need to automate your tests,
unit tests, integration tests,

00:32:26.790 --> 00:32:29.790
end-to-end tests, and
rerun them really often

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:31.500
to catch regressions.

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:34.200
At Google, we basically
run every related test

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:35.577
on every code change.

00:32:35.577 --> 00:32:38.160
And we simply block the change
from ever being committed if it

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:40.500
doesn't pass all of our tests.

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:42.270
This is a high bar to pass.

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:45.460
But it also gives us
a lot of confidence

00:32:45.460 --> 00:32:48.950
that, when we put code out,
it's not going to blow up.

00:32:48.950 --> 00:32:51.120
And you want
continuous delivery so

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:53.700
that you can get new code
out as often as possible.

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:57.240
This way each release will be
nice and small, which means

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:59.803
smaller things can go wrong.

00:32:59.803 --> 00:33:02.220
It's much easier for you to
figure out what is going wrong

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:04.770
when there's only a few
changes per release.

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.050
Now, I want to talk about
something really important

00:33:07.050 --> 00:33:08.700
with continuous delivery here.

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:11.880
And that's gradual rollouts.

00:33:11.880 --> 00:33:15.660
Gradual rollouts allow you to
discover problems and roll back

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:18.990
before all your
users are affected.

00:33:18.990 --> 00:33:21.990
The alternative here is just
shipping your entire binary

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:24.540
and putting it out to
all services at once.

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:26.430
This gets the new code
out there quickly.

00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:30.390
But if there's a problem,
you're now 100% down.

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:33.912
A gradual rollout will only
affect a few people at a time.

00:33:33.912 --> 00:33:35.370
And you can compare
the error rates

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:38.340
for those few people against
the control for all the people

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:41.010
on the previous version and
see if those error rates,

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:42.767
the percentages, increase.

00:33:42.767 --> 00:33:44.850
And once you have a good
statistically significant

00:33:44.850 --> 00:33:46.860
number of them, you
can say, OK, we're

00:33:46.860 --> 00:33:49.312
reasonably confident that
this new release is bad.

00:33:49.312 --> 00:33:51.270
Let's roll it back even
though we haven't fully

00:33:51.270 --> 00:33:52.300
rolled it out yet.

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:54.330
It saves a lot of
your error budget.

00:33:54.330 --> 00:33:58.200
It makes that slope of the graph
at the beginning go down a lot

00:33:58.200 --> 00:33:59.280
more gradually.

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:02.350
And that's a very good thing.

00:34:02.350 --> 00:34:03.780
We call this canary analysis.

00:34:07.930 --> 00:34:10.690
All this filters back
to the dev side as well.

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:12.580
Since there's always
two active versions

00:34:12.580 --> 00:34:14.489
of the code in
production, you need

00:34:14.489 --> 00:34:16.480
your data structures
and all your code

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:19.010
to be both forward and
backward compatible.

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:20.710
Why both forward and backward?

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:22.659
Because you may get
partially rolled out,

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:25.755
change some of your back end
data, and then roll back.

00:34:25.755 --> 00:34:27.130
So you need your
previous version

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:29.650
to be able to read the data
written by your next version

00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:31.100
and vice versa.

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:32.679
So whenever you
make a change, make

00:34:32.679 --> 00:34:35.085
sure they can read up
to two versions back.

00:34:35.085 --> 00:34:37.210
And whenever you're expecting
a change to be there,

00:34:37.210 --> 00:34:38.650
make sure it's there for
at least two versions

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:39.525
before you change it.

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:46.889
Next is about your service
architecture overall.

00:34:46.889 --> 00:34:50.330
So what makes an
architecture robust?

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:52.000
Your architecture
is robust if it

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:55.199
continues serving your users
despite significant internal

00:34:55.199 --> 00:34:56.489
failures.

00:34:56.489 --> 00:35:00.420
Think redundancy, no
single points of failure,

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:02.020
graceful degradation.

00:35:02.020 --> 00:35:04.200
This means reduced
functionality when

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:06.570
there's an outage instead
of no functionality

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:09.720
when there's an outage like,
for example, if you serve

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:11.910
some data gathered
from some back end

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:13.740
and that back end goes down.

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:15.810
The page that you
display to users

00:35:15.810 --> 00:35:18.660
can still successfully be
shown to users but maybe

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:20.370
with stale data
or maybe just not

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:22.750
without that particular
piece of data.

00:35:22.750 --> 00:35:25.470
Make sure you write
your user facing

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:28.410
code such that it can
handle partial completions

00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:29.160
from the back end.

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:36.010
And finally, chaos engineering--
you hear about this a lot

00:35:36.010 --> 00:35:37.298
nowadays.

00:35:37.298 --> 00:35:39.340
Now, I want to say I don't
really like this name.

00:35:39.340 --> 00:35:40.900
And Gary said this before, too.

00:35:40.900 --> 00:35:43.090
We're not trying
to create chaos.

00:35:43.090 --> 00:35:46.027
We're trying to
create resilience.

00:35:46.027 --> 00:35:47.860
Whenever you see the
word chaos engineering,

00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:50.980
think resilience engineering.

00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:52.580
Anyway, why use it?

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:55.950
It's so you can find problems
under controlled conditions

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:59.290
so you don't find them in
uncontrolled conditions.

00:35:59.290 --> 00:36:04.177
If you think your architecture
is robust, poke it and verify.

00:36:04.177 --> 00:36:06.010
When I talked before
about disaster recovery

00:36:06.010 --> 00:36:09.100
training or testing, it was
for training the people,

00:36:09.100 --> 00:36:12.520
but you can also use it to
test the systems this way.

00:36:12.520 --> 00:36:15.430
And the cool thing is
it can all be automated.

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:18.100
Just write a test to break
something in production.

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:19.780
And what do you expect?

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:23.550
You expect that nothing
happens, at least to users.

00:36:23.550 --> 00:36:26.410
Internally, things might be
happening all over the place.

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:29.210
You automatically failover.

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:31.210
Your redundancy kicks in.

00:36:31.210 --> 00:36:32.590
This is all expected.

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:34.690
But your service
remains within your SLO.

00:36:34.690 --> 00:36:36.235
And that's the important part.

00:36:36.235 --> 00:36:37.110
Sometimes it doesn't.

00:36:37.110 --> 00:36:38.780
Sometimes you find
a real problem.

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:40.530
That's why you do this
in the first place.

00:36:45.420 --> 00:36:49.430
So what are the most important
things we've covered today?

00:36:49.430 --> 00:36:52.350
Pretty much all of them are
ignore at your own peril.

00:36:52.350 --> 00:36:54.960
But I think the most important
ones are the two at the top--

00:36:54.960 --> 00:36:56.810
SLOs and blameless culture.

00:36:56.810 --> 00:36:59.630
This is what underpins
everything else.

00:36:59.630 --> 00:37:02.720
Your SLOs give you that
common language to say,

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:03.640
we're out of SLO.

00:37:03.640 --> 00:37:04.850
We need to freeze.

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:07.310
You, the PM, you told
me in the beginning what

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:11.450
you need the user reliability
to be, and we're out of that.

00:37:11.450 --> 00:37:13.730
So you, as the PM, need to
work with the devs and say,

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:16.290
we need to prioritize the
reliability to get back up.

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:20.000
Your SLOs are your main
force of communication

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:22.190
across different disciplines.

00:37:22.190 --> 00:37:24.860
And blameless culture--
so you can make things

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:26.220
better so you can communicate.

00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:28.637
So you can work together and
not be in fear of each other.

00:37:28.637 --> 00:37:32.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

